<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barbary Coast</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Barbary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Barbary Coast was the name used by Europeans from the sixteenth to the nineteenth century for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>. The term derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>. Its ports were home to the Barbary corsairs, who raided shipping and coasts and took captives for ransom or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> until the French conquest of Algiers in 1830 ended the practice.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barbary-coast.jpg" alt="Barbary Coast" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dirk Jansz van Santen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B13_057-BARBARIA.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Barbary Coast, also called Barbary, Berbery or the Berber Coast, covered the coastal areas of central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, more precisely the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and the Ottoman borderlands. It was never a single political entity. From the sixteenth century it was divided into four states, from west to east: the Alawi Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and the Regencies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>. Their main rulers were the sultan of Morocco, the dey of Algiers, the bey of Tunis and the pasha of Tripoli. The name comes from an exonym for the Berbers, now called Amazigh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> was an economic lifeline of the Barbary states. Most captives came from piracy at sea and from raids on coasts around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, although there were also Atlantic raids as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>. In 1625 the pirate fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, by far the largest, numbered 100 ships carrying 8,000 to 10,000 men, and the corsair industry accounted for a quarter of the city's workforce. The corsairs were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> but also Turks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, Syrians and renegade Italians, especially Corsicans.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> fought back after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>. Ferdinand II launched campaigns to curb Barbary piracy and took North African cities including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melilla">Melilla</a>. In the seventeenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, now including expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a>, adopted European naval tactics. The Moriscos of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a> used their knowledge of Spanish shores to raid the Spanish Levante, and from 1617 raids struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, which led Spain to form a fleet around 1621, with limited success.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> fought the Barbary Wars from 1801 to 1805 against some of the states, leading to the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derna">Derna</a>, the second overseas military land action of the United States. The Second Barbary War ended with an agreement that American ships had free passage without paying tribute. Slaving came to an end in the early 1830s after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-conquest-of-algeria">French conquest of Algeria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Barbary Coast” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Order of Saint Stephen in 1561, a crusading order of knights based at Pisa, whose galleys fought the Ottomans and the <b>Barbary</b> corsairs; Tuscan galleys served in the Christian fleet at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571, and in 1607 the knights sacked Bona on the Algerian coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Mediterranean, galleys from Spain and its Italian kingdoms fought the Ottomans and <b>Barbary</b> corsairs, and a Holy League fleet under Don John of Austria won the Battle of Lepanto in 1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Spain the kingdom supplied soldiers, money and galleys for the Habsburg wars, including ships at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571, and viceroys built chains of coastal watchtowers against <b>Barbary</b> corsairs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Plague, malaria and <b>Barbary</b> raids along the coasts kept the population low; a severe epidemic in 1652–1656 killed a large share of the inhabitants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>For three centuries Algiers was the most powerful of the <b>Barbary</b> states and a frontier bastion of the Ottoman Empire in the western Mediterranean; its defeat of Charles V in 1541 and its campaigns against Spanish outposts halted Iberian expansion in the central Maghreb, while its ships reinforced…</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1528 Andrea Doria’s galleys served Charles V against the Ottomans and the <b>Barbary</b> corsairs, but in later centuries Genoa’s small navy could not protect it from the French bombardment of 1684.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
